## Decision: Terraform will be structured so that each folder represents one or more AWS
resources.

Resources will be structured so that it's easy to determine the AWS resource
type. 

For example the below folders and files represent the [Terraform data
center](0033-terraform-data-center.md).

```
data-center/
  aws/
    load-balancer/
      index.tf
      output.tf
      variables.tf
    ec2/
      index.tf
      output.tf
      variables.tf
```

Within each resource folder the `index.tf` has the main Terraform code for that
resource, the other files contain helper code and other Terraform files that add
additional support for the main resource.

For example the [index.tf for an EC2 resource](0034-ec2-resource.md):

```
provider {
  host = aws.host
}

resources {
  instance = {
    ec2 = {
      image = aws.image
    }
  }
}
```

The [index.tf for the ELB resource](0035-elb-resource.md):

```
provider {
  host = aws.host
}

resources {
  load-balancer = {
    elb = {
      listener = {
        port = aws.port
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Resources will be grouped with Terraform `data-source` modules so that common data
sources can be used within multiple files without having to repeat the data source
content.

For example the [ELB resource in the data-center module](0034-elb-resource-in-data-center.md):

```
provider {
  host = aws.host
}

data-source {
  elb-listener-data-source {
    listener = {
      port = aws.port
    }
  }
}
```

## Decision: Instead of trying to use Apollo for v5, we should instead build our own framework that solves the issues we had with Apollo. By building our own framework we can be more apt to use the framework ourselves.

The first few prototypes can have an initial format like:

```js
const graphql = {
  query: {
    id: (id => { // optional
      const idBlock = [
        { idBlock: id }
      ]
      // optional
      // add more data to be parsed
      // return new Date()
    },
  },
  resolve: {
    id: (id => { // optional
      const idBlock = [
        { idBlock: id }
      ]
      // optional
      // add more data to be parsed
      // return new Date()
    },
  },
  args: {
    // optional
    // add more args here
  },
}
```

The GraphQL types we should include are (but not limited to)

- Int, double, boolean, string, list, enum, custom

It should provide a simple and robust way for the v5 client and components to build queries.

Components can then use the types of the GraphQL types to query the component.

In the case of a mutation this would have a similar format:

```js
const graphql = {
  query: {
    id: (id => { // optional
      const idBlock = [
        { idBlock: id }
      ]
      // optional
      // add more data to be parsed
      // return new Date()
    },
  },
  resolve: {
    id: (id => { // optional
      const idBlock = [
        { idBlock: id }
      ]
      // optional
      // add more data to be parsed
      // return new Date()
    },
  },
  args: {
    // optional
    // add more args here
  },
}
```

Here the args object would be the mutation variables.

From this we can use a simple parser that can retrieve the arguments, parse them according to the types, and build the query accordingly. This query can then be executed to fetch the data.

It should then be possible to implement a similar resolver for a mutation query.

## Decision: The injection of context into log entries and the asynchronous forwarding of logs will be provided by a dedicated "handlers" interface (like the logging interface).

A handler is responsible for forwarding a log entry (the return value of `log(logger, ...)`) and adding any additional context to it.

```java
interface Handler<T : Logger, TContextType, TDestinationType> {

    // method to forward a log entry to another handler
    T forward(T entry, TContextType ctx, TDestinationType dest);

    // method to inject a log entry to a logging group (like the Logger in the example above)
    // use the same format (e.g. "2020-06-08T12:53:55.666Z [DEBUG-0] log(...)")
    // the "0" in this example means a log was written by the main thread, not the logging group
    T debug(T entry, TContextType ctx, TDestinationType dest);

}
```

